<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period (907–979) was an era of political division in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. Short-lived dynasties succeeded one another in the north while several kingdoms ruled the south. It ended with the reunification of China by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period.jpg" alt="Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Dynasties_Ten_Kingdoms_923_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> of 755 to 763, large regional administrations called circuit commissions were laid over the old prefectures, and they became the boundaries of the later southern regimes. By the early 10th century the Tang government had given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a>, the regional military governors, such powers that they were de facto independent, with hereditary succession and armies that rivalled the palace armies. Many states were independent long before 907, and the key event was their recognition as sovereign by foreign powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Five dynastic states followed one another in the Central Plain: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, Later Tang, Later Jin, Later Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>. The term Five Dynasties was coined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> historians and reflects the view that the successive regimes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. Zhu Wen founded the first after forcing the last Tang emperor to abdicate in his favour, and the rest and the Song itself emerged from a military organisation led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a>. The Later Zhou under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong</a> conquered much of Southern Tang between 956 and 958. In 960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Zhao Kuangyin</a> staged a coup and founded the Northern Song, the official end of the Five Dynasties.</p><p>In the south more than a dozen states existed at once, of which ten are traditionally called the Ten Kingdoms. They included Wuyue, Min, Southern Han, Former Shu, Later Shu, Jingnan, Southern Tang and Northern Han. Their founders were mostly men of low social status who rose through military ability. The south was more stable than the north, and its kingdoms promoted trade, land reclamation and infrastructure. Commerce grew so fast that bank drafts called flying money partly met a shortage of metal currency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> subdued the remaining southern states. It swept away Jingnan in 963, Later Shu in 965, Southern Han in 971 and Southern Tang in 975, and Wuyue gave up its land in 978. The last regime, Northern Han, held out until the Song conquered it in 979. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> remained in the north.</p><p>Chronicles written from the northern viewpoint, the Old History and the New History, organised the period around the Five Dynasties and presented the Ten Kingdoms as illegitimate. The period turned away from the international mood of the Tang and was a transition towards the national culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and the Song adopted a distinctively southern Chinese cultural outlook. The tradition that it was a time of judicial abuse reflects both real problems and the bias of Confucian historians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Northern Song (960–1127) reunified most of the Chinese heartland after the fragmentation of the <b>Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The ensuing <b>Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</b> (907–960) was a prolonged political fragmentation, yet it preserved much of the Tang institutional and cultural inheritance and encouraged the commercial and agricultural developments that the Song would systematise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>They sent tribute missions to the <b>Five Dynasties</b> courts and the Song Dynasty, intermarried with the Cao rulers of the Guiyi Circuit at Dunhuang and sometimes fought them.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganzhou-uyghur-kingdom">Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Though nominally a Tang and later <b>Five Dynasties</b> province, it was a hereditary oasis state, first under the Zhang family and from 914 under the Cao family, who married into the Khotanese and Uyghur royal houses.</blockquote><a href="/state/guiyi-circuit">Guiyi Circuit</a></li><li><blockquote>Jin thus became the Later Tang, and the Shatuo Turks went on to found three of the <b>Five Dynasties</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li></ul></section>
